From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: sun60i-a733: deliver the root port's AER, PME and bwctrl interrupt

The root port binds three port services -- PME, AER and bwctrl -- and all
three sat on one MSI vector that never fired. Measured on the 6 GB board on
2026-09-21 (Gen3 x1 NVMe under load): Root Status shows
CERcvd+ MultCERcvd+, Error Source ERR_COR 0100, Root Command reports every
class, and the vector stayed at 0 on every CPU with aer_dev_correctable at 0.

WHY THE MSI NEVER COMES

The DesignWare core does not send its own root port's events as an MSI. It
raises them on sideband outputs, each in two flavours (UM V1.00 18.3.8.62,
SII_INT_0, printed pages 1229-1236):

  cfg_aer_rc_err_msi / _int     bit 2 / bit 1
  cfg_pme_msi / _int            bit 4 / bit 3
  hp_msi / hp_int               bit 15 / bit 14
  cfg_link_auto_bw_msi / _int   bit 17 / bit 16
  cfg_bw_mgt_msi / _int         bit 19 / bit 18

The _msi output is a one-cycle pulse while the port's MSI is enabled; it
fires regardless of the vector's mask bit, and honouring that mask is the
application's job. The _int output is a level while INTx is enabled;
for AER the UM says outright that it is for use with MSI/MSI-X disabled,
and points to cfg_aer_rc_err_msi otherwise. Either way the SoC has to turn
the output into an interrupt, and the A733 has one path for it: SII_INT_0,
unmasked through SII_INT_MASK_0 (18.3.8.60, 1 = not masked, reset 0), onto
PCIE0_SII, INTID 184 = SPI 152 (chapter 12).
The MSI receiver terminates inbound MSI that arrives over the link (Table
18-4); the port's own pulse never goes there, which is what the silicon
showed.

Another DesignWare integration in the tree records the same fact:
drivers/pci/quirks.c turns MSI off on the Tegra194 root ports ("Tegra's PCIe
Root Ports don't generate MSI interrupts for PME and AER events; instead only
INTx interrupts are generated").

THE FIX

The root port runs on INTx, and the SoC's SII line is its INTx:

 - The DT gains the PCIE0_SII interrupt, a legacy-interrupt-controller
   child, and a node for the root port itself (pcie@0,0, reg 00:00.0) whose
   interrupts-extended names hwirq 0 of that controller. of_irq_parse_pci()
   takes a device's own node first (drivers/pci/of.c, of_irq_parse_one()),
   so the root port resolves there. The two "of_irq_parse_pci: failed with
   rc=-22" lines came from the root port and the NVMe walking up to the GIC
   with a one-cell PCI pin; the root port's is gone.
 - There is deliberately no host-level interrupt-map. With one, the
   endpoint's INTA resolved to the same hwirq -- of_irq_parse_pci() builds
   the lookup address from the root port's devfn -- and an INTx-only
   endpoint or pci=nomsi would have received an interrupt that fires only on
   root-port events. Without it an endpoint's lookup fails as before and
   dev->irq stays 0: a clean failure instead of a hang.
 - The driver chains the SII line into a one-entry domain. Unmasking hwirq 0
   opens the five _int bits above. The handler clears the latch, then
   dispatches, and after each pass re-reads the root port's own status and
   enable pairs through the DBI -- AER Root Error Status against Root Error
   Command, Root Status PME against Root Control, Link Status LBMS/LABS
   against Link Control -- and dispatches again while one is pending, at
   most four passes. The UM does not say whether an SII_INT_0 bit follows
   its output's level or latches only a rising edge. If edge-only, two
   overlapping events keep the level high with no second edge (AER writes
   back only the Root Error Status bits it read), and a handler that trusts
   the latch alone would go deaf until reboot. The re-read makes it correct
   under both; which one the silicon has is measured on the board.
 - The port's MSI and MSI-X capabilities are unlinked from its capability
   list with dw_pcie_remove_capability(). portdrv puts any port that offers
   MSI on MSI, and then the core only pulses outputs nobody listens to.
   Doing it in the driver scopes it to this compatible; a PCI quirk would
   have to key on the port's ID, 1f6d:abcd, which is a placeholder.

PREMISE, MEASURED

On the current image (without this patch), 2026-09-21:
SII_INT_0 (0x06400e08) = 0x200A0004 with SII_INT_MASK_0 = 0: bit 2
(cfg_aer_rc_err_msi) and bits 17/19 (the bandwidth _msi pulses) latched while
masked, and AER Root Error Status = 0x3. The outputs reach the SII latch;
nothing had opened the mask or claimed the line.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO

Downstream INTx stays unserved, as before. SII_INT_0 latches Assert_INTx and
Deassert_INTx as separate pulses (bits 5-12), so with both latched in one
read the state of the wire is unknowable. MSI and MSI-X endpoints -- the NVMe
included -- are untouched: they stay on the DWC MSI receiver, SPI 153.

VENDOR

The vendor BSP (pcie-sunxi-rc.c / pcie-sunxi-plat.c, read as a hint) requests
the same SII line but unmasks only the Assert_INTx pulses and treats each as
an edge, ignoring Deassert, so a second interrupt raised before the first is
acknowledged is lost. It unmasks no root-port event bit and leaves MSI on the
port, so its AER and PME are as deaf as ours were. Its DT carries a four-pin
host-level interrupt-map, which this patch leaves out on purpose (above); the
register semantics come from the UM, not from it. A running vendor 6.6 kernel on an A733 board holds the same line as
"pcie-sii" on SPI 152 -- a hint that the wiring matches, not a source.

VERIFIED

Applies at fuzz 0 and builds (compile-check, driver and dtb). The dtb carries
interrupts SPI 152 + 153 named "sii", "msi", no interrupt-map or
#interrupt-cells on the host node, and pcie@0,0 with interrupts-extended to
the legacy-interrupt-controller, hwirq 0. NOT hardware-proven. The
observables that prove or falsify it, on the board with the NVMe:
/proc/interrupts shows "A733-PCIe-INTx 0 Level ... PCIe PME, aerdrv, PCIe
bwctrl"; SII_INT_MASK_0 reads 0x5400a; after clearing the root port's
Correctable Error Status and generating read load, that line's count and
aer_dev_correctable both rise.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-sun60i-a733.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-sun60i-a733.c
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-sun60i-a733.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-sun60i-a733.c
@@ -12,9 +12,11 @@
  *
  *   UDS + 0x0c00  PCIE_LTSSM_ENABLE   bit0 starts link training,
  *                                     [7:4] device type, reset value 4 = RC
+ *   UDS + 0x0e00  SII_INT_MASK_0      1 passes an SII_INT_0 bit to the SII line
+ *   UDS + 0x0e08  SII_INT_0           the root port's own event outputs, W1C
  *   UDS + 0x0e0c  SII_INT_1           bit0 SMLH link up, bit1 RDLH link up
  *
- * Both documented in the A733 UM V1.00, PCIe chapter 18.3, which tabulates the
+ * All documented in the A733 UM V1.00, PCIe chapter 18.3, which tabulates the
  * whole User Defined Space. The DBI base itself is NOT where the UM prints it:
  * the module base in the memory map is the UDS at 0x06400000, and the DBI sits
  * 0x400000 below at 0x06000000 -- measured on hardware,
@@ -36,6 +38,8 @@
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/gpio/consumer.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/irqchip/chained_irq.h>
+#include <linux/irqdomain.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/moduleparam.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
@@ -56,6 +60,44 @@
 #define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_RDLH_LINK_UP		BIT(1)
 
 /*
+ * The System Information Interface: the DesignWare core's sideband outputs,
+ * latched by Allwinner's glue into write-1-to-clear status registers and ORed
+ * onto one GIC line, PCIE0_SII (UM V1.00 18.3.8.60-18.3.8.67; INTID 184 =
+ * SPI 152 in chapter 12). A mask bit of 1 passes its status bit to that line,
+ * 0 blocks it, and every mask resets to 0.
+ */
+#define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_0		0x0e00
+#define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_1		0x0e04
+#define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_0		0x0e08
+#define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_2		0x0e10
+#define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_3		0x0e14
+
+/*
+ * The root port's own interrupt, in its INTx form: the level outputs the core
+ * asserts while the port's Command register has INTx enabled and the event's
+ * status and enable bits are both set -- AER (Root Error Status), PME (Root
+ * Status), hot-plug (Slot Status) and the two link-bandwidth notifications
+ * (Link Status). SII_INT_0 bits 1, 3, 14, 16 and 18, UM V1.00 18.3.8.62.
+ */
+#define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_AER_RC_ERR_INT	BIT(1)
+#define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_PME_INT		BIT(3)
+#define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_HP_INT		BIT(14)
+#define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_LINK_AUTO_BW_INT	BIT(16)
+#define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_BW_MGT_INT		BIT(18)
+/*
+ * Passes through the root port's services per SII interrupt before the handler
+ * gives up; see sun60i_a733_pcie_rp_pending().
+ */
+#define SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_MAX_PASSES		4
+
+#define  SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_RP_EVENTS				\
+	(SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_AER_RC_ERR_INT |			\
+	 SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_PME_INT |				\
+	 SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_HP_INT |				\
+	 SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_LINK_AUTO_BW_INT |		\
+	 SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_BW_MGT_INT)
+
+/*
  * PCIe CEM 3.0: at least 100 ms between the reference clock being valid and
  * PERST# being released, and the spec's T_RRS before the first config request.
  * dw_pcie_wait_for_link() covers the second; this covers the first.
@@ -90,6 +132,11 @@
 	struct phy		*phy;
 	struct gpio_desc	*perst;
 	struct regulator	*vpcie3v3;
+	struct irq_domain	*rp_domain;
+	raw_spinlock_t		sii_lock;
+	int			sii_irq;
+	u8			exp_cap;
+	u16			aer_cap;
 };
 
 static struct sun60i_a733_pcie *to_sun60i_a733_pcie(struct dw_pcie *pci)
@@ -144,12 +191,225 @@
 }
 
 /*
- * Empty, and required: dw_pcie_host_init() dereferences pp->ops without
- * checking it (pcie-designware-host.c, `if (pp->ops->init)`), so a NULL here is
- * an oops during probe rather than a default. Nothing in this controller needs
- * a host callback -- the core's own init path does everything.
+ * The root port's event interrupt.
+ *
+ * The DesignWare core does not deliver its own root port's AER, PME or
+ * link-bandwidth events as an MSI. It signals them on sideband outputs, in two
+ * flavours: a one-cycle "_msi" pulse while the port's MSI capability is
+ * enabled, and a level "_int" while INTx is enabled (UM V1.00 18.3.8.62, and
+ * for the AER output the UM says it is for use with MSI/MSI-X
+ * disabled, else cfg_aer_rc_err_msi is used). Turning either flavour into
+ * an interrupt is left to the SoC, and on the A733 the only path is SII_INT_0
+ * onto the PCIE0_SII line. Nothing turns the pulse into a write to the MSI
+ * receiver: measured on 2026-09-21, with the port's AER, PME and bwctrl
+ * services on an MSI vector, Root Status recorded correctable errors and the
+ * vector never fired.
+ *
+ * So the root port runs on INTx. Its Interrupt Pin is INTA (dw_pcie_setup_rc()
+ * writes 1), and the root port's own DT node names hwirq 0 of the one-entry
+ * domain below as its interrupt; the SII handler feeds that hwirq from the
+ * five level outputs. The port's MSI and MSI-X capabilities are unlinked from
+ * its capability list, because a port that advertises MSI is put on MSI by
+ * portdrv, and the core then only pulses outputs nobody listens to.
+ *
+ * Downstream INTx is deliberately not served. SII_INT_0 latches the
+ * Assert_INTx and Deassert_INTx messages as separate one-cycle pulses; with
+ * both latched in one read, their order -- whether the line is up or down --
+ * is lost. There is no host-level interrupt-map, so an INTx-only endpoint gets
+ * no IRQ rather than one that never fires. MSI and MSI-X endpoints are
+ * unaffected.
+ */
+
+/*
+ * Whether the root port still has an event its services have not taken: the
+ * same status-and-enable pairs that drive the core's _int outputs, read back
+ * from the port's own config space.
+ *
+ * Why this is needed: the UM does not say whether an SII_INT_0 bit follows its
+ * output's level or latches only its rising edge. If it is edge-only, a level
+ * that stays high produces no second edge. That happens as soon as two events
+ * overlap -- AER, for one, writes back only the Root Error Status bits it read,
+ * so an error class arriving in between keeps cfg_aer_rc_err_int high -- and
+ * the port would go deaf until reboot. Re-reading the source after each pass
+ * makes the handler correct under both behaviours; which one the silicon has
+ * is still to be measured.
  */
+static bool sun60i_a733_pcie_rp_pending(struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie)
+{
+	struct dw_pcie *pci = &pcie->pci;
+	u32 cmd, sta, lnk;
+
+	if (pcie->aer_cap) {
+		cmd = dw_pcie_readl_dbi(pci, pcie->aer_cap + PCI_ERR_ROOT_COMMAND);
+		sta = dw_pcie_readl_dbi(pci, pcie->aer_cap + PCI_ERR_ROOT_STATUS);
+		if ((sta & PCI_ERR_ROOT_COR_RCV) &&
+		    (cmd & PCI_ERR_ROOT_CMD_COR_EN))
+			return true;
+		if ((sta & PCI_ERR_ROOT_UNCOR_RCV) &&
+		    (cmd & (PCI_ERR_ROOT_CMD_NONFATAL_EN |
+			    PCI_ERR_ROOT_CMD_FATAL_EN)))
+			return true;
+	}
+
+	if (pcie->exp_cap) {
+		cmd = dw_pcie_readw_dbi(pci, pcie->exp_cap + PCI_EXP_RTCTL);
+		sta = dw_pcie_readl_dbi(pci, pcie->exp_cap + PCI_EXP_RTSTA);
+		if ((sta & PCI_EXP_RTSTA_PME) && (cmd & PCI_EXP_RTCTL_PMEIE))
+			return true;
+
+		/* Link Control in the low half, Link Status in the high. */
+		lnk = dw_pcie_readl_dbi(pci, pcie->exp_cap + PCI_EXP_LNKCTL);
+		if (((lnk >> 16) & PCI_EXP_LNKSTA_LBMS) &&
+		    (lnk & PCI_EXP_LNKCTL_LBMIE))
+			return true;
+		if (((lnk >> 16) & PCI_EXP_LNKSTA_LABS) &&
+		    (lnk & PCI_EXP_LNKCTL_LABIE))
+			return true;
+	}
+
+	return false;
+}
+
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_sii_handler(struct irq_desc *desc)
+{
+	struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie = irq_desc_get_handler_data(desc);
+	struct irq_chip *chip = irq_desc_get_chip(desc);
+	int pass;
+
+	chained_irq_enter(chip, desc);
+
+	if (readl(pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_0) &
+	    readl(pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_0) &
+	    SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_RP_EVENTS) {
+		for (pass = 0; pass < SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_MAX_PASSES; pass++) {
+			/*
+			 * Clear the latch, then look at the source, then
+			 * dispatch: an event that arrives after the look
+			 * latches anew and raises the line again, so none
+			 * falls between the two.
+			 */
+			writel(SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_RP_EVENTS,
+			       pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_0);
+			if (pass && !sun60i_a733_pcie_rp_pending(pcie))
+				break;
+			generic_handle_domain_irq(pcie->rp_domain, 0);
+		}
+		if (pass == SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_MAX_PASSES &&
+		    sun60i_a733_pcie_rp_pending(pcie))
+			dev_warn_ratelimited(pcie->pci.dev,
+					     "root port event still pending after %d passes\n",
+					     pass);
+	}
+
+	chained_irq_exit(chip, desc);
+}
+
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_sii_update(struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie,
+					bool enable)
+{
+	unsigned long flags;
+	u32 val;
+
+	raw_spin_lock_irqsave(&pcie->sii_lock, flags);
+	val = readl(pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_0);
+	if (enable)
+		val |= SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_RP_EVENTS;
+	else
+		val &= ~SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_RP_EVENTS;
+	writel(val, pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_0);
+	raw_spin_unlock_irqrestore(&pcie->sii_lock, flags);
+}
+
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_intx_mask(struct irq_data *d)
+{
+	sun60i_a733_pcie_sii_update(irq_data_get_irq_chip_data(d), false);
+}
+
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_intx_unmask(struct irq_data *d)
+{
+	sun60i_a733_pcie_sii_update(irq_data_get_irq_chip_data(d), true);
+}
+
+static struct irq_chip sun60i_a733_pcie_intx_chip = {
+	.name		= "A733-PCIe-INTx",
+	.irq_mask	= sun60i_a733_pcie_intx_mask,
+	.irq_unmask	= sun60i_a733_pcie_intx_unmask,
+	.flags		= IRQCHIP_SKIP_SET_WAKE | IRQCHIP_MASK_ON_SUSPEND,
+};
+
+static int sun60i_a733_pcie_intx_map(struct irq_domain *domain,
+				     unsigned int irq, irq_hw_number_t hwirq)
+{
+	irq_set_chip_and_handler(irq, &sun60i_a733_pcie_intx_chip,
+				 handle_level_irq);
+	irq_set_chip_data(irq, domain->host_data);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static const struct irq_domain_ops sun60i_a733_pcie_intx_domain_ops = {
+	.map = sun60i_a733_pcie_intx_map,
+	.xlate = irq_domain_xlate_onecell,
+};
+
+static int sun60i_a733_pcie_host_init(struct dw_pcie_rp *pp)
+{
+	struct dw_pcie *pci = to_dw_pcie_from_pp(pp);
+	struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie = to_sun60i_a733_pcie(pci);
+	struct device *dev = pci->dev;
+	struct device_node *intc;
+
+	/*
+	 * Every mask closed and every root-port status cleared before the
+	 * line is live: the masks reset to 0, but a warm start finds whatever
+	 * the boot loader left behind.
+	 */
+	writel(0, pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_0);
+	writel(0, pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_1);
+	writel(0, pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_2);
+	writel(0, pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_3);
+	writel(SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_RP_EVENTS,
+	       pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_0);
+
+	/* See the root port's event interrupt above: it runs on INTx. */
+	dw_pcie_remove_capability(pci, PCI_CAP_ID_MSI);
+	dw_pcie_remove_capability(pci, PCI_CAP_ID_MSIX);
+
+	pcie->exp_cap = dw_pcie_find_capability(pci, PCI_CAP_ID_EXP);
+	pcie->aer_cap = dw_pcie_find_ext_capability(pci, PCI_EXT_CAP_ID_ERR);
+
+	intc = of_get_child_by_name(dev->of_node, "legacy-interrupt-controller");
+	if (!intc)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, -EINVAL,
+				     "no legacy-interrupt-controller node\n");
+
+	/* One source, the root port: hwirq 0, and nothing else can map. */
+	pcie->rp_domain = irq_domain_create_linear(of_fwnode_handle(intc), 1,
+						   &sun60i_a733_pcie_intx_domain_ops,
+						   pcie);
+	of_node_put(intc);
+	if (!pcie->rp_domain)
+		return dev_err_probe(dev, -ENOMEM, "INTx domain\n");
+
+	irq_set_chained_handler_and_data(pcie->sii_irq,
+					 sun60i_a733_pcie_sii_handler, pcie);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+static void sun60i_a733_pcie_host_deinit(struct dw_pcie_rp *pp)
+{
+	struct dw_pcie *pci = to_dw_pcie_from_pp(pp);
+	struct sun60i_a733_pcie *pcie = to_sun60i_a733_pcie(pci);
+
+	writel(0, pcie->app + SUN60I_A733_PCIE_SII_INT_MASK_0);
+	irq_set_chained_handler_and_data(pcie->sii_irq, NULL, NULL);
+	irq_domain_remove(pcie->rp_domain);
+}
+
 static const struct dw_pcie_host_ops sun60i_a733_pcie_host_ops = {
+	.init	= sun60i_a733_pcie_host_init,
+	.deinit	= sun60i_a733_pcie_host_deinit,
 };
 
 static const struct dw_pcie_ops sun60i_a733_dw_pcie_ops = {
@@ -274,6 +534,11 @@
 	if (IS_ERR(pcie->app))
 		return PTR_ERR(pcie->app);
 
+	raw_spin_lock_init(&pcie->sii_lock);
+	pcie->sii_irq = platform_get_irq_byname(pdev, "sii");
+	if (pcie->sii_irq < 0)
+		return pcie->sii_irq;
+
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(pcie->clks); i++)
 		pcie->clks[i].id = sun60i_a733_pcie_clks[i];
 	ret = devm_clk_bulk_get(dev, ARRAY_SIZE(pcie->clks), pcie->clks);
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -2015,8 +2015,14 @@
 			 */
 			ranges = <0x81000000 0x0 0x21000000 0x0 0x21000000 0x0 0x01000000>,
 				 <0x82000000 0x0 0x22000000 0x0 0x22000000 0x0 0x06000000>;
-			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 153 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
-			interrupt-names = "msi";
+			/*
+			 * PCIE0_SII (INTID 184) carries the root port's own AER,
+			 * PME and link events; PCIE0_MSI (INTID 185) is the DWC
+			 * MSI receiver for the endpoints. UM V1.00 chapter 12.
+			 */
+			interrupts = <GIC_SPI 152 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>,
+				     <GIC_SPI 153 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+			interrupt-names = "sii", "msi";
 			clocks = <&ccu CLK_PCIE_AUX>,
 				 <&ccu CLK_PCIE_AXI_SLV>,
